window.__courseChapter("v1-11", {"v1-11-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **Hardware Acceleration** (Vol I). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, Purpose", "md": "Arithmetic is inexpensive relative to data movement. In the time required to fetch a single scalar operand from off-chip DRAM, modern arithmetic units can execute dozens to hundreds of floating-point operations, and that DRAM access consumes on the order of a hundred times more energy than the computation itself. This imbalance, the memory wall, means specialized accelerators are not merely faster arithmetic engines."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "Anatomy of an Accelerator", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "Hardware Specialization", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "Compute Primitives", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "Compute Units", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "Memory Systems", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "Performance Bounds", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Hardware Mapping", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**Accelerator anatomy**", "Every ML accelerator is matrix, vector, and special-function units beside on-chip memory, a wide off-chip path, and a host link, and it wins by spending on dense arithmetic and local storage the transistors a CPU spends on speculation and coherence, which pays only when the workload exposes the supported operation, precision, and parallelism."], ["**Matching primitives to execution units**", "Learned layers reduce to element-wise vector work, matrix multiply, and special functions, and each maps to the unit whose reuse matches it, so routing a primitive to the wrong unit collapses throughput to a fraction of peak."], ["**Operand-supply hierarchy**", "Delivered throughput is set by where operands live, with registers and SRAM supplying the array at its rate while HBM, host memory over PCIe, and flash fall off a bandwidth taper, so the diagnostic is traffic across each boundary, not capacity alone."], ["**Energy ladder**", "A DRAM access costs on the order of a hundred times an arithmetic operation or an on-chip SRAM read, so data movement, not arithmetic, is the primary energy target, and locality structures such as systolic arrays earn their area by avoiding the costliest event on the ladder."], ["**Amdahl's law**", "Acceleration speeds only the fraction p of baseline time on the accelerated path, so speedup is 1/((1-p) + p/A) and never exceeds 1/(1-p), and the unaccelerated fraction is defined by the intervention (data loading, host overhead, unsupported operators, launches)."], ["**Roofline and ridge point**", "Attainable performance is the minimum of peak rate and bandwidth times arithmetic intensity, the ridge point (peak over bandwidth) marks where a kernel turns compute-bound, and intensity depends on implementation and batch, so the roofline bounds performance while profiling names the mechanism below the bound."], ["**Stationary dataflows**", "Every matmul operand is reused along a different loop and local storage can hold only one cheaply, so the loop order decides which operand stays (weight-, output-, or input-stationary) and two identical schedules can differ many-fold in traffic."], ["**Tiling**", "Tiling partitions an operation into blocks that fit a fast memory tier so each block is reused before eviction, with tile size bounded by every live buffer and dimensions off the tile grid paying a fringe penalty."], ["**Compilers and runtimes**", "ML compilers apply graph rewrites, kernel selection, memory planning by liveness, and scheduling over a mapping space too large for hand tuning, and runtimes dispatch precompiled tactics within declared shape ranges."], ["**Heterogeneous SoCs and CMOS power**", "Phone and embedded chips put CPU, GPU, DSP, and NPU on one shared memory system under a few-watt envelope managed by DVFS, where dynamic power scales roughly as frequency cubed, which is why accelerators favor wide arrays at moderate clocks."], ["**Choosing an accelerator**", "No datasheet chooses a machine because the limiting resource belongs to the workload on that machine, so cost and energy are divided by throughput delivered on the limiting resource, not by advertised peak."], ["**Multi-chip scaling within a node**", "Past one accelerator each added boundary lowers bandwidth and raises latency, so partitioning pays only when saved compute exceeds exposed communication (awareness in Vol I)."], ["**Reduced-precision and sparse datapaths**", "Narrower formats help twice, fewer bytes per operand and more multiply-accumulate units per area, but only on a supported kernel with a wide accumulator, and 2:4 sparse units stream compressed weights plus position metadata for an advertised 2x."], ["**Fusion limits on accelerators**", "Fusion on an accelerator is limited by register pressure, occupancy, and reductions, so a fused kernel that holds intermediates on chip trades on-chip capacity for removed memory traffic."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$\\text{Speedup} = \\frac{1}{(1 - p) + \\frac{p}{S}}$$\n\n$$B_{\\text{hw}} = \\frac{R_{\\text{peak}}}{\\text{BW}}$$\n\n$$T_{\\text{mem}} = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}}$$\n\n$$T_{\\text{compute}} = \\frac{\\text{FLOPs}}{R_{\\text{peak}}}$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v1-11-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "Accelerator anatomy", "back": "Every ML accelerator is matrix, vector, and special-function units beside on-chip memory, a wide off-chip path, and a host link, and it wins by spending on dense arithmetic and local storage the transistors a CPU spends on speculation and coherence, which pays only when the workload exposes the supported operation, precision, and parallelism."}, {"front": "Matching primitives to execution units", "back": "Learned layers reduce to element-wise vector work, matrix multiply, and special functions, and each maps to the unit whose reuse matches it, so routing a primitive to the wrong unit collapses throughput to a fraction of peak."}, {"front": "Operand-supply hierarchy", "back": "Delivered throughput is set by where operands live, with registers and SRAM supplying the array at its rate while HBM, host memory over PCIe, and flash fall off a bandwidth taper, so the diagnostic is traffic across each boundary, not capacity alone."}, {"front": "Energy ladder", "back": "A DRAM access costs on the order of a hundred times an arithmetic operation or an on-chip SRAM read, so data movement, not arithmetic, is the primary energy target, and locality structures such as systolic arrays earn their area by avoiding the costliest event on the ladder."}, {"front": "Amdahl's law", "back": "Acceleration speeds only the fraction p of baseline time on the accelerated path, so speedup is 1/((1-p) + p/A) and never exceeds 1/(1-p), and the unaccelerated fraction is defined by the intervention (data loading, host overhead, unsupported operators, launches)."}, {"front": "Roofline and ridge point", "back": "Attainable performance is the minimum of peak rate and bandwidth times arithmetic intensity, the ridge point (peak over bandwidth) marks where a kernel turns compute-bound, and intensity depends on implementation and batch, so the roofline bounds performance while profiling names the mechanism below the bound."}, {"front": "Stationary dataflows", "back": "Every matmul operand is reused along a different loop and local storage can hold only one cheaply, so the loop order decides which operand stays (weight-, output-, or input-stationary) and two identical schedules can differ many-fold in traffic."}, {"front": "Tiling", "back": "Tiling partitions an operation into blocks that fit a fast memory tier so each block is reused before eviction, with tile size bounded by every live buffer and dimensions off the tile grid paying a fringe penalty."}, {"front": "Compilers and runtimes", "back": "ML compilers apply graph rewrites, kernel selection, memory planning by liveness, and scheduling over a mapping space too large for hand tuning, and runtimes dispatch precompiled tactics within declared shape ranges."}, {"front": "Heterogeneous SoCs and CMOS power", "back": "Phone and embedded chips put CPU, GPU, DSP, and NPU on one shared memory system under a few-watt envelope managed by DVFS, where dynamic power scales roughly as frequency cubed, which is why accelerators favor wide arrays at moderate clocks."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Accelerator anatomy**?", "options": ["A DRAM access costs on the order of a hundred times an arithmetic operation or an on-chip SRAM read, so data movement, not arithmetic, is the primary energy target, and locality structures such as systolic arrays earn their area by avoiding the costliest event on the ladder.", "Fusion on an accelerator is limited by register pressure, occupancy, and reductions, so a fused kernel that holds intermediates on chip trades on-chip capacity for removed memory traffic.", "No datasheet chooses a machine because the limiting resource belongs to the workload on that machine, so cost and energy are divided by throughput delivered on the limiting resource, not by advertised peak.", "Every ML accelerator is matrix, vector, and special-function units beside on-chip memory, a wide off-chip path, and a host link, and it wins by spending on dense arithmetic and local storage the transistors a CPU spends on speculation and coherence, which pays only when the workload exposes the supported operation, precision, and parallelism."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Accelerator anatomy:** Every ML accelerator is matrix, vector, and special-function units beside on-chip memory, a wide off-chip path, and a host link, and it wins by spending on dense arithmetic and local storage the transistors a CPU spends on speculation and coherence, which pays only when the workload exposes the supported operation, precision, and parallelism.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Accelerator anatomy itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Matching primitives to execution units**?", "options": ["Narrower formats help twice, fewer bytes per operand and more multiply-accumulate units per area, but only on a supported kernel with a wide accumulator, and 2:4 sparse units stream compressed weights plus position metadata for an advertised 2x.", "ML compilers apply graph rewrites, kernel selection, memory planning by liveness, and scheduling over a mapping space too large for hand tuning, and runtimes dispatch precompiled tactics within declared shape ranges.", "Learned layers reduce to element-wise vector work, matrix multiply, and special functions, and each maps to the unit whose reuse matches it, so routing a primitive to the wrong unit collapses throughput to a fraction of peak.", "Delivered throughput is set by where operands live, with registers and SRAM supplying the array at its rate while HBM, host memory over PCIe, and flash fall off a bandwidth taper, so the diagnostic is traffic across each boundary, not capacity alone."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Matching primitives to execution units:** Learned layers reduce to element-wise vector work, matrix multiply, and special functions, and each maps to the unit whose reuse matches it, so routing a primitive to the wrong unit collapses throughput to a fraction of peak.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Matching primitives to execution units itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Operand-supply hierarchy**?", "options": ["ML compilers apply graph rewrites, kernel selection, memory planning by liveness, and scheduling over a mapping space too large for hand tuning, and runtimes dispatch precompiled tactics within declared shape ranges.", "Phone and embedded chips put CPU, GPU, DSP, and NPU on one shared memory system under a few-watt envelope managed by DVFS, where dynamic power scales roughly as frequency cubed, which is why accelerators favor wide arrays at moderate clocks.", "Every matmul operand is reused along a different loop and local storage can hold only one cheaply, so the loop order decides which operand stays (weight-, output-, or input-stationary) and two identical schedules can differ many-fold in traffic.", "Delivered throughput is set by where operands live, with registers and SRAM supplying the array at its rate while HBM, host memory over PCIe, and flash fall off a bandwidth taper, so the diagnostic is traffic across each boundary, not capacity alone."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Operand-supply hierarchy:** Delivered throughput is set by where operands live, with registers and SRAM supplying the array at its rate while HBM, host memory over PCIe, and flash fall off a bandwidth taper, so the diagnostic is traffic across each boundary, not capacity alone.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Operand-supply hierarchy itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Energy ladder**?", "options": ["Fusion on an accelerator is limited by register pressure, occupancy, and reductions, so a fused kernel that holds intermediates on chip trades on-chip capacity for removed memory traffic.", "A DRAM access costs on the order of a hundred times an arithmetic operation or an on-chip SRAM read, so data movement, not arithmetic, is the primary energy target, and locality structures such as systolic arrays earn their area by avoiding the costliest event on the ladder.", "Every matmul operand is reused along a different loop and local storage can hold only one cheaply, so the loop order decides which operand stays (weight-, output-, or input-stationary) and two identical schedules can differ many-fold in traffic.", "No datasheet chooses a machine because the limiting resource belongs to the workload on that machine, so cost and energy are divided by throughput delivered on the limiting resource, not by advertised peak."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Energy ladder:** A DRAM access costs on the order of a hundred times an arithmetic operation or an on-chip SRAM read, so data movement, not arithmetic, is the primary energy target, and locality structures such as systolic arrays earn their area by avoiding the costliest event on the ladder.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Energy ladder itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Amdahl's law**?", "options": ["A DRAM access costs on the order of a hundred times an arithmetic operation or an on-chip SRAM read, so data movement, not arithmetic, is the primary energy target, and locality structures such as systolic arrays earn their area by avoiding the costliest event on the ladder.", "Acceleration speeds only the fraction p of baseline time on the accelerated path, so speedup is 1/((1-p) + p/A) and never exceeds 1/(1-p), and the unaccelerated fraction is defined by the intervention (data loading, host overhead, unsupported operators, launches).", "Fusion on an accelerator is limited by register pressure, occupancy, and reductions, so a fused kernel that holds intermediates on chip trades on-chip capacity for removed memory traffic.", "No datasheet chooses a machine because the limiting resource belongs to the workload on that machine, so cost and energy are divided by throughput delivered on the limiting resource, not by advertised peak."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Amdahl's law:** Acceleration speeds only the fraction p of baseline time on the accelerated path, so speedup is 1/((1-p) + p/A) and never exceeds 1/(1-p), and the unaccelerated fraction is defined by the intervention (data loading, host overhead, unsupported operators, launches).", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Amdahl's law itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["Accelerator anatomy: Every ML accelerator is matrix, vector, and special-function units beside on-chip memory, a wide off-chip path, and a host link, and it wins by spending on dense arithmetic and local storage the transistors a CPU spends on speculation and coherence, which pays only when the workload exposes the supported operation, precision, and parallelism.", "Matching primitives to execution units: Learned layers reduce to element-wise vector work, matrix multiply, and special functions, and each maps to the unit whose reuse matches it, so routing a primitive to the wrong unit collapses throughput to a fraction of peak.", "Operand-supply hierarchy: Delivered throughput is set by where operands live, with registers and SRAM supplying the array at its rate while HBM, host memory over PCIe, and flash fall off a bandwidth taper, so the diagnostic is traffic across each boundary, not capacity alone.", "Energy ladder: A DRAM access costs on the order of a hundred times an arithmetic operation or an on-chip SRAM read, so data movement, not arithmetic, is the primary energy target, and locality structures such as systolic arrays earn their area by avoiding the costliest event on the ladder."]}], "terms": [], "id": "v1-11-00-overview", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v1-11-01-anatomy-of-an-accelerator": {"title": "Anatomy of an Accelerator", "summary": "Calculate end-to-end acceleration speedups using Amdahl's Law given workload parallel fractions.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Anatomy of an Accelerator"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Anatomy of an Accelerator** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate end-to-end acceleration speedups using Amdahl's Law given workload parallel fractions\n- Compare why a compute-focused hardware upgrade benefits high-reuse training kernels more than low-reuse batch-1 decoding\n- Analyze how hardware interventions redefine the accelerated versus unaccelerated components of execution time\n- Identify arithmetic intensity from its FLOP-per-byte definition and use it to reason about modeled hardware ceilings\n- Classify which part of the iron law is most directly affected by moving a GEMM onto a supported mixed-precision Tensor Core path"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Anatomy of an Accelerator", "md": "The same INT8 weights can run far faster on one execution path than on another, because the hardware that executes a model, not the model itself, determines how much of every compression saving survives execution. @Sec-model-compression closed on a deployment profile for ResNet-50 quantized to INT8 (a later section): a model four times smaller whose large-batch throughput rises severalfold on the reference H100 while its batch-one latency barely moves."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: Iron law of ML systems decomposition into data transfer, compute, and latency; Amdahl's Law and the acceleration wall for compute vs memory bound workloads; Parallel fractions and speedup ceilings across workload archetypes; Arithmetic intensity diagnostic and hardware balance introduction."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why a compute-only accelerator upgrade can help high-reuse convolutional training more than batch-1 autoregressive decoding, even when the new device advertises much higher peak arithmetic throughput $R_{\\text{peak}}$.", "md": "High-reuse convolutional training can often turn loaded weights and activations into many arithmetic operations before eviction, so raising supported compute throughput can materially lift the achievable ceiling when the profile is already near the compute side of the roofline. Batch-1 autoregressive decoding instead tends to stream large weights with little cross-sample reuse, so increasing $R_{\\text{peak}}$ alone may leave runtime dominated by memory traffic, host overhead, or other unaccelerated work. The practical implication is that the same hardware upgrade must be evaluated against the workload's measured baseline fractions and arithmetic intensity, not against peak FLOP/s alone."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An ML engineer upgrades a training cluster from baseline CPUs to NVIDIA H100 GPUs, providing an accelerator compute gain of G_accel = 250× on matrix operations. If data preprocessing, serialization, and CPU-GPU transfers constitute \\$10\\% of the baseline execution time (p = 0.90), what is the maximum achievable end-to-end speedup according to Amdahl's Law?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. By Amdahl's Law, total speedup is Speedup = frac1(1-p) + fracpG_accel = frac10.10 + frac0.90250 = frac10.10 + 0.0036 = frac10.1036 approx 9.65×. The theoretical ceiling as G_accel to infty is frac11-p = frac10.10 = 10×. The linear scaling choice overlooks the non-accelerated \\$10\\% serial fraction that dominates execution time once compute is accelerated. The intermediate values of \\$25× and \\$125× arbitrarily assume partial scaling without applying the (1-p) bottleneck constraint.", "type": "single", "options": ["Approximately \\$9.6\\times$ (bounded by the theoretical ceiling of \\$10\\times$)", "Approximately \\$25× (bounded by the memory bandwidth ratio)", "Approximately \\$125× (half of the matrix unit throughput multiplier)", "Approximately \\$250× (scaling linearly with the raw compute gain)"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: calculate end-to-end acceleration speedups using Amdahl's Law given workload parallel fractions."]}, {"q": "In the iron law of ML systems decomposition ($\\text{Time} = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}} + \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}} + L_{\\text{lat}}$), the unaccelerated fraction $(1-p)$ is an immutable, fixed property of the model architecture regardless of the hardware intervention.", "explanation": "False. The unaccelerated fraction $(1-p)$ is defined specifically by the hardware or system intervention rather than being an immutable architectural constant. For example, if an upgrade improves only the tensor computation unit ($R_{\\text{peak}}$), data transfer ($D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW}$) acts as the unaccelerated fraction; however, if the memory subsystem or interconnect is also upgraded, data transfer time is reduced and the relative unaccelerated fraction shifts.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how hardware interventions redefine the accelerated versus unaccelerated components of execution time."]}, {"q": "The metric defined as the ratio of floating-point operations performed to bytes of memory traffic transferred across a specific memory hierarchy interface is called ____.", "explanation": "arithmetic intensity. Arithmetic intensity is measured in FLOP/byte and, at a chosen memory interface, helps identify which modeled roofline ceiling is lower; profiling is still needed to confirm the actual bottleneck.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["arithmetic intensity"], "hints": ["Think about: identify arithmetic intensity from its FLOP-per-byte definition and use it to reason about modeled hardware ceilings."]}, {"q": "In the iron law of ML systems, end-to-end execution latency is modeled as $\\text{Time} = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}} + \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}} + L_{\\text{lat}}$. Which term is most directly improved when replacing standard FP32 GEMM instructions with a supported mixed-precision Tensor Core path for the same operation?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. A supported mixed-precision Tensor Core path raises the relevant arithmetic-throughput ceiling for that matrix operation, so it most directly improves the compute term via a higher applicable $R_{\\text{peak}}$. Realized efficiency can also improve if the kernel maps well, but that is not automatic. The PCIe answer confuses on-chip execution with host transfer volume, the HBM-bandwidth answer confuses compute units with memory-interface capability, and the driver-latency answer targets a fixed overhead rather than the matrix-multiply execution path.", "type": "single", "options": ["Only the data volume $D_{\\text{vol}}$ transferred over PCIe", "Only the fixed invocation latency $L_{\\text{lat}}$ of the kernel driver", "Only the memory interface bandwidth $\\text{BW}$ of the HBM subsystem", "The compute term through higher relevant $R_{\\text{peak}}$, while realized $\\eta_{\\text{hw}}$ may improve but is not guaranteed"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: classify which part of the iron law is most directly affected by moving a GEMM onto a supported mixed-precision Tensor Core path."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate end-to-end acceleration speedups using Amdahl's Law given workload parallel fractions.", "You can now compare why a compute-focused hardware upgrade benefits high-reuse training kernels more than low-reuse batch-1 decoding.", "You can now analyze how hardware interventions redefine the accelerated versus unaccelerated components of execution time.", "You can now identify arithmetic intensity from its FLOP-per-byte definition and use it to reason about modeled hardware ceilings."]}], "id": "v1-11-01-anatomy-of-an-accelerator", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-02-hardware-specialization": {"title": "Hardware Specialization", "summary": "Explain the physical breakdown of Dennard scaling that led to domain-specific acceleration.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Hardware Specialization", "Specialized computing", "Domain-specific architectures", "Machine learning hardware specialization", "The integration bottleneck"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Hardware Specialization** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain the physical breakdown of Dennard scaling that led to domain-specific acceleration\n- Compare software-managed scratchpad memory with hardware-managed caches for deep learning workloads\n- Explain the historical evolution of hardware specialization from FPUs to modern AI accelerators\n- Evaluate the architectural source of energy efficiency in domain-specific accelerators like TPUv1\n- Explain why the divergence between model compute demand and single-device hardware scaling drives specialization"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Hardware Specialization", "md": "General-purpose processors dedicate substantial silicon area and power to instruction fetch, branch prediction, out-of-order scheduling, and cache coherence, machinery built for arbitrary control flow that yields poor arithmetic density on regular, dense linear algebra. When Google deployed its first Tensor Processing Unit (TPU v1) into production data centers alongside contemporary CPUs and NVIDIA K80 GPUs, stripping away that general-purpose control machinery in favor of a dedicated matrix array produced an immediate efficiency jump (example \\ref{exmp-hw-acceleration-tpuv1-vs-k80-efficiency-shock})."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Specialized computing\n- Domain-specific architectures\n- Machine learning hardware specialization\n- The integration bottleneck\n\nPay attention to: Physical breakdown of Dennard scaling and the rise of dark silicon; Emergence of Domain-Specific Architectures (DSAs) and technology S-curves; Model compute demand outrunning single-device hardware scaling; Software-managed scratchpads vs hardware-managed caches in the integration bottleneck."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Contrast the architectural trade-offs of software-managed scratchpad memory (such as Google TPUv1's Unified Buffer) with hardware-managed cache hierarchies when executing large-scale tensor workloads.", "md": "Software-managed scratchpads eliminate the hardware overhead of tag arrays, cache line state machines, coherence snooping, and dynamic replacement policies, allowing more silicon area and power to be dedicated to storage capacity and dense arithmetic units. When compilers can statically determine regular tensor access patterns, they can deterministically tile and stage data. However, scratchpads increase compiler complexity and software burden, and they perform poorly on irregular, dynamically indexed memory patterns that hardware caches manage automatically."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "What primary physical limitation brought about the end of Dennard scaling in the mid-2000s, necessitating the transition from increasing CPU clock frequencies to domain-specific hardware accelerators?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Dennard scaling postulated that as transistors shrank, operating voltage could drop in proportion, keeping chip power density constant while operating frequencies increased. When threshold voltage and leakage currents prevented further voltage scaling around 2005, increasing clock frequency resulted in prohibitive power density and thermal dissipation limits (the power wall and 'dark silicon'), forcing architects to seek energy efficiency through architectural specialization and parallelism. The lithography choice misidentifies lithography limits as the cause of the mid-2000s frequency stall. The quantum tunneling choice confuses gate oxide leakage with bulk copper transmission. The semiconductor depletion choice is physically fabricated.", "type": "single", "options": ["Lithography light diffraction preventing further reduction of transistor gate length below \\$1\\,mum", "Inability to lower operating voltage proportionally with transistor size, leading to unsustainable power density and heat dissipation limits", "Quantum tunneling in copper interconnect lines preventing data transmission between arithmetic units", "Depletion of global silicon substrate supplies requiring migration to gallium nitride semiconductors"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: explain the physical breakdown of Dennard scaling that led to domain-specific acceleration."]}, {"q": "Place the following computing milestones in chronological order (from earliest to most recent) as hardware evolved toward modern AI accelerators:", "explanation": "The correct order is (3) Integration of floating-point units (FPUs) and digital signal processors (DSPs) as discrete coprocessors -> (4) General-purpose programmable GPUs and SIMD instruction set extensions for 3D graphics and multimedia -> (2) Emergence of fixed-function media codecs and network processors for video/packet streaming -> (1) Introduction of dedicated Tensor Cores and TPUs for deep learning matrix operations. During the 1980s, discrete FPUs and DSPs accelerated arithmetic. The 1990s introduced SIMD extensions and early 3D GPUs. The 2000s expanded fixed-function media engines and network processors. The 2010s ushered in deep learning DSAs like Google TPUs (2015/2017) and NVIDIA Volta Tensor Cores (2017).", "type": "order", "items": ["General-purpose programmable GPUs and SIMD instruction set extensions for 3D graphics and multimedia", "Emergence of fixed-function media codecs and network processors for video/packet streaming", "Introduction of dedicated Tensor Cores and TPUs for deep learning matrix operations"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the historical evolution of hardware specialization from FPUs to modern AI accelerators."]}, {"q": "Google's TPUv1 achieved substantial performance-per-watt improvements over contemporary general-purpose CPUs primarily by operating at significantly higher clock frequencies.", "explanation": "False. TPUv1 operated at a modest clock frequency (approximately \\$700 MHz) and achieved \\$30×--80× higher performance-per-watt than contemporary CPUs by omitting complex general-purpose features (such as out-of-order execution, branch prediction, and multi-level cache coherence) and dedicating silicon area to a massive \\$256×256 INT8 systolic matrix multiplier and a large software-managed Unified Buffer.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the architectural source of energy efficiency in domain-specific accelerators like TPUv1."]}, {"q": "Why has the pressure to build specialized ML hardware grown since the 2012 deep learning breakthrough?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The measured training-compute trend doubles on the order of 3.4 to 6 months, far faster than the roughly two-year doubling of transistor density, so single-device scaling alone cannot close the gap; specialization, parallelism, and hardware-software co-design must close the rest. The other options describe real but unrelated mismatches that do not explain the growth in specialization pressure.", "type": "single", "options": ["Consumer mobile SoCs began to exceed data center GPUs in memory bandwidth", "On-chip SRAM latency rose relative to host DRAM latency over PCIe", "Training compute demand has doubled every few months while transistor density doubles roughly every two years, so no single chip can keep pace without parallelism and co-design", "Python framework dispatch overhead grew faster than GPU kernel execution time"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: explain why the divergence between model compute demand and single-device hardware scaling drives specialization."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain the physical breakdown of Dennard scaling that led to domain-specific acceleration.", "You can now compare software-managed scratchpad memory with hardware-managed caches for deep learning workloads.", "You can now explain the historical evolution of hardware specialization from FPUs to modern AI accelerators.", "You can now evaluate the architectural source of energy efficiency in domain-specific accelerators like TPUv1."]}], "id": "v1-11-02-hardware-specialization", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-03-compute-primitives": {"title": "Compute Primitives", "summary": "Classify deep learning mathematical operations to their corresponding hardware execution units.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Compute Primitives", "Vector operations", "Matrix operations", "Special function units"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Compute Primitives** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Classify deep learning mathematical operations to their corresponding hardware execution units\n- Explain the mechanism and memory trade-offs of the im2col transformation for accelerating convolutions\n- Compare the low arithmetic intensity of element-wise vector operations with the higher reuse available in matrix multiplication\n- Explain how implicit GEMM eliminates memory duplication overhead in convolution acceleration\n- Classify neural network layers based on operational reuse and hardware execution affinity"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Compute Primitives", "md": "A single forward pass through a neural network layer can issue millions or billions of MAC operations, and executing them one scalar instruction at a time would swamp the processor in instruction-fetch and register-access overhead. Across fully connected, convolutional, and attention layers, learned linear transformations reduce to these repeated MACs, whose regular tensor structure exposes the parallel work and operand reuse that hardware specialization requires."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Vector operations\n- Matrix operations\n- Special function units\n\nPay attention to: Vector operations: element-wise math, reductions, and memory boundedness; Matrix operations: dense GEMM, attention projections, and convolution mapping via im2col / implicit GEMM; Special Function Units (SFUs) for transcendental activations and normalizations; Mapping neural network computational graphs to appropriate execution primitives."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain how the $\\text{im2col}$ (image-to-column) transformation allows standard 2D convolution operations to execute on high-throughput matrix multiplication hardware (GEMM engines), and describe the primary memory overhead associated with this approach.", "md": "The $\\text{im2col}$ transformation extracts overlapping local receptive field patches from an input tensor and flattens each patch into a column (or row) of a 2D matrix, while flattening the convolution filters into a weight matrix. This converts the sliding-window convolution into a standard GEMM ($C = A \\cdot B$) that executes efficiently on Tensor Cores or systolic arrays. However, because receptive fields overlap, input pixel values are duplicated across multiple patches, expanding memory footprint by up to roughly the filter area ($K_h \\times K_w$) unless implemented via implicit-GEMM address calculation without physical materialization."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "When executing a Transformer layer containing attention projection ($Q = X W_q$), Softmax ($\\text{Softmax}(S)$), Layer Normalization ($\\text{LayerNorm}(X)$), and GeLU activation ($\\text{GeLU}(Z)$), which execution unit is specifically responsible for computing transcendental functions (exponential and error function approximations)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Special Function Units (SFUs) are specialized hardware pipelines designed to evaluate transcendental and nonlinear mathematical functions—such as exponentials in Softmax, reciprocal square roots in LayerNorm, and Gaussian error approximations in GeLU—using hardware lookup tables and polynomial approximations. Matrix units and Tensor Cores are dedicated to high-throughput matrix multiply-accumulate operations, not transcendental approximations. Vector Load-Store Memory Controllers handle memory address generation and data movement across caches and DRAM.", "type": "single", "options": ["Systolic 2D Matrix Multiply Units", "Dense Tensor Cores", "Vector Load-Store Memory Controllers", "Special Function Units (SFUs)"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: classify deep learning mathematical operations to their corresponding hardware execution units."]}, {"q": "In modern AI accelerators, element-wise vector operations such as residual additions ($Y = X_1 + X_2$) achieve higher arithmetic intensity than large matrix-matrix multiplications ($C = A \\cdot B$).", "explanation": "False. A residual addition does very little arithmetic per byte moved; under a simple FP32 traffic model with two reads and one write, it is about \\$1/12 FLOP/byte. Large matrix multiplication can reuse loaded tiles many times, so its arithmetic intensity is much higher even though measured performance still depends on implementation and memory behavior.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: compare the low arithmetic intensity of element-wise vector operations with the higher reuse available in matrix multiplication."]}, {"q": "The hardware transformation technique that enables convolutional layers to execute as matrix multiplications without physically duplicating overlapping patch data in memory is known as ____ GEMM.", "explanation": "implicit. Implicit GEMM computes input tensor memory addresses on-the-fly inside kernel index calculations during tile loading, avoiding the large $\\mathcal{O}(K_h \\cdot K_w)$ memory expansion of explicit im2col materialization while retaining matrix-unit execution efficiency.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["implicit"], "hints": ["Think about: explain how implicit GEMM eliminates memory duplication overhead in convolution acceleration."]}, {"q": "Which of the following operations in a modern deep learning architecture exhibits the highest operational arithmetic reuse, making it most suitable for dense 2D systolic arrays and Tensor Cores?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Batched linear layers multiply an activation matrix by a weight matrix, enabling each weight element to be reused across all batch elements and each activation element to be reused across multiple output features, yielding high arithmetic intensity suitable for 2D matrix units. Element-wise ReLU performs only one comparison per element load. Batch Normalization mean computation performs reduction passes over data with minimal arithmetic reuse per byte transferred. Embedding table lookups are gather operations with zero arithmetic operations per byte fetched.", "type": "single", "options": ["Batched linear layer matrix multiplication ($Y = X W$)", "Element-wise ReLU activation ($\\max(0, x)$)", "Channel-wise Batch Normalization mean computation", "Token-wise embedding table lookup"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: classify neural network layers based on operational reuse and hardware execution affinity."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now classify deep learning mathematical operations to their corresponding hardware execution units.", "You can now explain the mechanism and memory trade-offs of the im2col transformation for accelerating convolutions.", "You can now compare the low arithmetic intensity of element-wise vector operations with the higher reuse available in matrix multiplication.", "You can now explain how implicit GEMM eliminates memory duplication overhead in convolution acceleration."]}], "id": "v1-11-03-compute-primitives", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-04-compute-units": {"title": "Compute Units", "summary": "Explain how 2:4 structured sparsity reduces transferred value payload and enables a higher dense-equivalent sparse Tensor Core path.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Compute Units", "From SIMD lanes to SIMT threads", "Tensor Cores", "Processing elements", "Systolic arrays", "Numerics and sparsity on the datapath"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Compute Units** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain how 2:4 structured sparsity reduces transferred value payload and enables a higher dense-equivalent sparse Tensor Core path\n- Sequence the workflow for 2:4 structured sparse matrix multiplication from constraint formation through runtime sparse execution\n- Compare FP16 and BF16 in terms of dynamic range versus fraction precision using the format properties introduced in the section\n- Analyze the performance penalty of warp divergence in SIMT architectures"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Compute Units", "md": "Applying ReLU to a 512-element vector shows why execution models matter. The operation is simple, but throughput depends on whether the hardware treats those 512 comparisons as scalar instructions, as lanes of a single instruction, multiple data (SIMD) unit, as GPU threads, or as elements of a tile that one tile-level program processes together. Modern AI processors package the three compute primitives into distinct execution units: vector units for vector work, matrix units such as Tensor Cores and systolic arrays for matrix work, and special function units for nonlinear functions."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- From SIMD lanes to SIMT threads\n- Tensor Cores\n- Processing elements\n- Systolic arrays\n- Numerics and sparsity on the datapath\n\nPay attention to: SIMD vs SIMT execution models and warp divergence penalties; Tensor Cores and mixed-precision matrix execution (FP16, BF16, FP8 E4M3 vs E5M2); N:M structured sparsity mechanics and 2:4 sparse Tensor Cores; Systolic array architectures and dataflow strategies (Weight-Stationary vs Output-Stationary)."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In NVIDIA's Ampere and Hopper architectures, how does 2:4 structured sparsity achieve an up to \\$2× dense-equivalent Tensor Core throughput path for supported matrix multiplication?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The 2:4 pattern keeps exactly two nonzeros in each contiguous four-value group along the supported dimension, so the stored value payload is cut roughly in half while compact metadata indicates which positions were kept. Sparse Tensor Cores then use that metadata to select matching activations and skip the removed values. The row-dropping idea describes a different pruning pattern, the four-thread explanation confuses SIMT scheduling with sparsity hardware, and the 2-bit-integer claim is quantization rather than structured sparsity.", "type": "single", "options": ["Exactly two non-zero values are preserved in every contiguous four-element block, so the value payload is halved while compact metadata records positions and sparse Tensor Cores compute only on the retained values", "Every alternate row of the weight matrix is dropped completely, allowing the GPU to halve the grid launch dimensions", "Four separate threads simultaneously execute one scalar multiply-accumulate instruction in a single clock cycle", "Floating-point numbers are converted to 2-bit integers, quadrupling register file capacity"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: explain how 2:4 structured sparsity reduces transferred value payload and enables a higher dense-equivalent sparse Tensor Core path."]}, {"q": "Place the following steps in the correct execution sequence for processing a matrix multiplication on an accelerator with Sparse Tensor Cores using 2:4 structured sparsity:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (1) Fine-tune or prune the weight matrix to ensure exactly two non-zero values exist in every four-element contiguous group, (4) Compress the sparse weight matrix by storing only the non-zero values alongside metadata describing their positions, (2) Sparse Tensor Cores load compressed weights and decode metadata to gather matching input activation elements, (3) Multiply non-zero weights by gathered activations and accumulate into output partial sums on the sparse path. The sequence is necessary because the runtime path assumes weights already satisfy the 2:4 constraint and have been compressed with consistent metadata. Swapping compression ahead of pruning would encode the wrong pattern, and executing the sparse multiply before metadata-guided selection would leave the hardware without the positional information needed to match retained weights with the correct activations.", "type": "order", "items": ["Fine-tune or prune the weight matrix to ensure exactly two non-zero values exist in every four-element contiguous group", "Compress the sparse weight matrix by storing only the non-zero values alongside metadata describing their positions", "Sparse Tensor Cores load compressed weights and decode metadata to gather matching input activation elements", "Multiply non-zero weights by gathered activations and accumulate into output partial sums on the sparse path"], "hints": ["Think about: sequence the workflow for 2:4 structured sparse matrix multiplication from constraint formation through runtime sparse execution."]}, {"q": "What is the primary numerical difference between FP16 and BF16 as presented in this section?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Both FP16 and BF16 are 16-bit floating-point formats, but BF16 keeps an 8-bit exponent like FP32, giving it a wider dynamic range, while FP16 devotes more bits to the stored fraction and therefore offers finer precision within a narrower range. The lower-byte-count claim is false because both use 16 bits, the integer claim is wrong because both are floating point, and the underflow claim is too strong because numerical behavior depends on the workload and execution path.", "type": "single", "options": ["FP16 uses fewer total bits than BF16, so it always transfers less data", "BF16 has a wider exponent range, while FP16 has more fraction precision within the same 16-bit width", "FP16 is an integer format, whereas BF16 is floating point", "BF16 always prevents gradient underflow, whereas FP16 cannot be used for training"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: compare FP16 and BF16 in terms of dynamic range versus fraction precision using the format properties introduced in the section."]}, {"q": "In NVIDIA's SIMT (Single Instruction, Multiple Threads) execution model, when threads within the same 32-thread warp execute divergent branches of an `if-else` condition, both paths are executed concurrently in parallel at full hardware throughput.", "explanation": "False. When threads within a warp diverge on conditional branches, the warp executes each branch path serially while masking off (disabling) threads that do not take that path. The total execution time becomes the sum of the times of both paths, reducing hardware utilization and throughput.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the performance penalty of warp divergence in SIMT architectures."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain how 2:4 structured sparsity reduces transferred value payload and enables a higher dense-equivalent sparse Tensor Core path.", "You can now sequence the workflow for 2:4 structured sparse matrix multiplication from constraint formation through runtime sparse execution.", "You can now compare FP16 and BF16 in terms of dynamic range versus fraction precision using the format properties introduced in the section.", "You can now analyze the performance penalty of warp divergence in SIMT architectures."]}], "id": "v1-11-04-compute-units", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-05-memory-systems": {"title": "Memory Systems", "summary": "Calculate model-state memory under a stated mixed-precision Adam byte recipe and identify why optimizer-related state dominates the footprint.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Memory Systems", "The AI memory wall", "Memory access patterns in ML workloads", "Memory hierarchy", "Host-accelerator communication", "Model memory pressure"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Memory Systems** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate model-state memory under a stated mixed-precision Adam byte recipe and identify why optimizer-related state dominates the footprint\n- Classify memory hierarchy levels by access latency and proximity to execution units\n- Explain the architectural design differences and physical advantages of HBM over GDDR\n- Explain the role of pinned host memory in optimizing host-to-accelerator data transfers\n- Distinguish memory-capacity limits from bandwidth bottlenecks in the AI memory wall"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Memory Systems", "md": "That gap between a … arithmetic floor and real inference latency opens whenever the memory subsystem cannot deliver weights and activations fast enough to keep the compute units busy. Modern accelerators provide hundreds of teraflops (or even petaflops) on low-precision tensor datapaths, yet their arithmetic pipelines idle as soon as memory traffic saturates the bus. This is the memory wall, the growing divergence between arithmetic capability and memory bandwidth, seen from inside the accelerator."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- The AI memory wall\n- Memory access patterns in ML workloads\n- Memory hierarchy\n- Host-accelerator communication\n- Model memory pressure\n\nPay attention to: The AI Memory Wall and hardware balance ridge point ($I_{\\text{ridge}} = R_{\\text{peak}}/\\text{BW}$); Memory hierarchy tiers, latencies, and bandwidths (Registers to HBM to Host DDR); HBM vs GDDR6X architectural differences (TSVs, silicon interposers); Host-accelerator communication (PCIe, pinned memory, DMA)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: When training a \\$7$-billion-parameter model using a hypothetical mixed-precision Adam recipe stated for this question—\\$2$ bytes for weights, \\$2$ for gradients, \\$4$ for FP32 master weights, \\$4$ for the first moment, and \\$4$ for the second moment per parameter—calculate the model-state memory for weights, gradients, and optimizer states, and explain why optimizer-related state dominates this footprint.", "md": "Using the provided recipe, each parameter carries \\$2 bytes for FP16/BF16 weights, \\$2 bytes for gradients, \\$4 bytes for FP32 master weights, \\$4 bytes for the first Adam moment, and \\$4 bytes for the second Adam moment, for a total of \\$16 bytes per parameter. A \\$7B-parameter model therefore requires \\$7 × 10^9 × 16 bytes = 112 GB for these model states alone. Of that total, optimizer-related state plus master weights account for \\$12 bytes per parameter, which is most of the footprint. The practical implication is that optimizer choice can dominate memory planning even before activations, workspace, or other runtime buffers are included."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Arrange the following levels of a modern GPU memory hierarchy in order of access latency, from lowest latency (fastest) to highest latency (slowest):", "explanation": "The correct order is (2) Register File -> (4) Shared Memory / L1 Cache -> (5) On-Chip L2 Cache -> (1) High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM3) -> (3) Pinned Host System Memory (DDR5 via PCIe). Registers are accessible in sub-nanosecond/single-cycle latency, followed by on-chip Shared Memory/L1 (~few cycles), L2 Cache (~tens of cycles), device HBM (~hundreds of cycles), and finally host DDR over the PCIe bus (~microseconds/thousands of cycles).", "type": "order", "items": ["Register File", "Shared Memory / L1 Cache", "On-Chip L2 Cache"], "hints": ["Think about: classify memory hierarchy levels by access latency and proximity to execution units."]}, {"q": "Why does High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) achieve significantly higher bandwidth (e.g., $>2\\text{ TB/s}$) than traditional GDDR6X memory (e.g., $\\approx 760\\text{ GB/s}$) while maintaining comparable or lower power per bit?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. HBM achieves ultra-high bandwidth by vertically stacking DRAM dies with Through-Silicon Vias (TSVs) and interfacing with the accelerator through a silicon interposer with extremely wide memory buses (1024 bits per stack vs. 32/64 bits for standard GDDR channels). Because physical wire distances over the silicon interposer are short and the bus is wide, HBM can run at lower pin clock frequencies, significantly reducing energy per bit ($pJ/\\text{bit}$) compared to driving high-frequency GDDR signals over long PCB traces. The higher clock frequency choice is incorrect because GDDR6X actually runs at higher pin clock frequencies than HBM. The optical photonics and error-correction elimination choices are physically false.", "type": "single", "options": ["HBM operates at a \\$10× higher clock frequency than GDDR6X on standard PCB traces", "HBM uses optical photonic signaling to transmit data across the motherboard", "HBM eliminates all error correction codes and row buffer precharge cycles", "HBM vertically stacks DRAM dies using Through-Silicon Vias (TSVs) and connects to the GPU via a wide 1024-bit per stack silicon interposer bus at lower clock speeds"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the architectural design differences and physical advantages of HBM over GDDR."]}, {"q": "Memory allocated on the host CPU that is locked into physical RAM and prevents operating system paging, enabling direct DMA transfers over PCIe to the GPU, is called ____ host memory.", "explanation": "pinned (or page-locked). Pinned host memory allows GPU DMA engines to transfer data asynchronously across the PCIe bus without CPU staging copies or page faults.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["pinned", "pinned (or page locked)", "pinned (or page-locked)"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the role of pinned host memory in optimizing host-to-accelerator data transfers."]}, {"q": "The AI Memory Wall refers solely to the limited physical capacity (GBs) of GPU DRAM, meaning that if an accelerator has sufficient gigabytes to store model weights, memory bandwidth will never bottleneck execution.", "explanation": "False. In this section, the AI memory wall is the gap between arithmetic throughput and memory bandwidth. Capacity is a separate feasibility constraint: a model can fit in memory and still run slowly if low-reuse kernels, such as LayerNorm or token-by-token decoding, are limited by bytes delivered per second rather than by available gigabytes.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish memory-capacity limits from bandwidth bottlenecks in the AI memory wall."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate model-state memory under a stated mixed-precision Adam byte recipe and identify why optimizer-related state dominates the footprint.", "You can now classify memory hierarchy levels by access latency and proximity to execution units.", "You can now explain the architectural design differences and physical advantages of HBM over GDDR.", "You can now explain the role of pinned host memory in optimizing host-to-accelerator data transfers."]}], "id": "v1-11-05-memory-systems", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-06-performance-bounds": {"title": "Performance Bounds", "summary": "Calculate the hardware ridge point from peak compute throughput and memory bandwidth.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Performance Bounds", "The roofline model", "Hardware ridge points", "Memory bandwidth bounds", "Optimization by intensity regime"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Performance Bounds** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate the hardware ridge point from peak compute throughput and memory bandwidth\n- Apply the roofline equation to compute a modeled upper bound and identify whether the bandwidth or compute ceiling is lower\n- Analyze why rising compute-to-bandwidth ratios push newer hardware ridge points upward and increase the need for reuse-aware kernel design\n- Evaluate why increasing peak compute alone does not raise the simple roofline bound for a memory-bound kernel\n- Explain the significance of the ridge point in roofline performance modeling"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Performance Bounds", "md": "The compute units fix how fast an accelerator can calculate, and the memory system fixes how fast it can be fed. A kernel's performance is bounded by whichever of the two runs out first. Putting numbers on that statement takes three pieces: the roofline's ceilings for each precision path, the ridge point at which they meet, and the hardware balance that moves the ridge from one generation to the next. The ResNet-50 INT8 workload meets its sharpest lesson in these bounds."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- The roofline model\n- Hardware ridge points\n- Memory bandwidth bounds\n- Optimization by intensity regime\n\nPay attention to: Roofline equation: Attainable Performance = min(R_peak, I * BW); Arithmetic intensity calculation and hardware ridge points; Memory-bound vs compute-bound regime diagnosis; Regime-specific optimization strategies (kernel fusion, tiling, ILP, quantization)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why has the hardware ridge point ($I_{\\text{ridge}}$) increased across successive GPU generations, and what pressure does this trend place on compiler and kernel developers?", "md": "The ridge point rises when peak arithmetic throughput grows faster than memory bandwidth, because $I_{\\text{ridge}} = R_{\\text{peak}}/\\text{BW}$. That means newer devices require more operations per byte to reach their compute ceiling on the same precision path. For example, a kernel that once cleared an older ridge may fall below a newer one if its reuse pattern does not improve. The practical consequence is that compilers and kernel authors face stronger pressure to preserve locality through layout choices, tiling, and fusion while comparing like-for-like precision paths instead of explaining the trend with lower-precision headline peaks alone."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An accelerator delivers $R_{\\text{peak}} = 1{,}000\\text{ TFLOP/s}$ (\\$10^{15}\\text{ FLOP/s}$) in FP16 and has a High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) subsystem delivering $\\text{BW} = 2.0\\text{ TB/s}$ (\\$2\\times 10^{12}\\text{ bytes/s}$). What is the hardware balance point (ridge point $I_{\\text{ridge}}$) of this system?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The ridge point is the hardware ratio $I_{\\text{ridge}} = \\frac{R_{\\text{peak}}}{\\text{BW}} = \\frac{10^{15}\\text{ FLOP/s}}{2.0 \\times 10^{12}\\text{ bytes/s}} = 500\\text{ FLOP/byte}$. That value marks where the simple roofline's compute and bandwidth ceilings are equal for this interface. It is a modeled threshold, not proof that a measured kernel actually reaches either ceiling; the other options come from arithmetic errors in the same ratio.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$50 FLOP/byte", "\\$200 FLOP/byte", "\\$500 FLOP/byte", "\\$2,000 FLOP/byte"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the hardware ridge point from peak compute throughput and memory bandwidth."]}, {"q": "A developer runs a LayerNorm kernel on an accelerator with $R_{\\text{peak}} = 312\\text{ TFLOP/s}$ and $\\text{BW} = 1.5\\text{ TB/s}$ ($I_{\\text{ridge}} = 208\\text{ FLOP/byte}$). The LayerNorm has an arithmetic intensity of $I = 4\\text{ FLOP/byte}$. What is the maximum attainable performance under this simple roofline model, and what modeled ceiling is lower?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The simple roofline gives $R_{\\text{attain}} = \\min(R_{\\text{peak}}, I \\cdot \\text{BW}) = \\min(312, 4 \\times 1.5) = 6.0\\text{ TFLOP/s}$. Because $I = 4$ FLOP/byte is far below the ridge point of 208 FLOP/byte, the lower modeled ceiling is the bandwidth line, not the compute peak. The compute-peak answer ignores arithmetic intensity, and the warp-scheduler and PCIe answers introduce bottlenecks that are not part of the stated roofline calculation. Profiling would still be needed to determine whether measured execution is actually limited by this interface ceiling or by additional overheads.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$6.0 TFLOP/s, the bandwidth ceiling", "\\$312 TFLOP/s, the compute ceiling", "\\$78 TFLOP/s, the warp-scheduler ceiling", "\\$1.5 TFLOP/s, the PCIe ceiling"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: apply the roofline equation to compute a modeled upper bound and identify whether the bandwidth or compute ceiling is lower."]}, {"q": "When a kernel operates in the memory-bound regime of the Roofline model ($I < I_{\\text{ridge}}$), doubling the accelerator's peak tensor compute capability ($R_{\\text{peak}}$) without changing memory bandwidth will double the kernel's execution speed.", "explanation": "False. In the simple roofline, a kernel below the ridge is bounded by $I \\cdot \\text{BW}$, so raising only $R_{\\text{peak}}$ lifts the compute ceiling but does not raise the bandwidth ceiling. That does not prove measured speedup is exactly zero in every implementation, but the modeled bandwidth bound itself is unchanged.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why increasing peak compute alone does not raise the simple roofline bound for a memory-bound kernel."]}, {"q": "In the Roofline model, the transition point on the horizontal axis where the memory-bandwidth ceiling intersects the peak-compute ceiling is known as the hardware ____ point.", "explanation": "ridge. The ridge point ($I_{\\text{ridge}} = R_{\\text{peak}} / \\text{BW}$) defines the minimum arithmetic intensity required for an algorithm to potentially reach peak hardware computational throughput.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["ridge"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the significance of the ridge point in roofline performance modeling."]}, {"q": "An engineer profiles a transformer inference workload and discovers that the attention Softmax kernel is heavily memory bandwidth bound. Which of the following optimization techniques directly targets intermediate-memory traffic in this kernel?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Fusing scale, mask, and Softmax can reduce intermediate global-memory traffic by keeping temporary values on chip when cache, register, and shared-memory resources permit. That targets the bytes-moved side of the problem directly, even though it does not guarantee the kernel becomes compute-bound. Upgrading host RAM changes a different interface, increasing Tensor Core clock raises the compute ceiling rather than the memory-traffic pattern, and disabling scheduler flexibility does not address the diagnosed bottleneck.", "type": "single", "options": ["Upgrading host CPU RAM to DDR5 to decrease kernel enqueue latency", "Fusing the scale, mask, and Softmax stages into a single kernel to keep intermediates in registers or SRAM when resources allow", "Increasing the clock frequency of the GPU Tensor Cores by \\$15\\%", "Disabling warp scheduler out-of-order instruction issue"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: select a memory-traffic-reduction optimization for a bandwidth-bound inference Softmax kernel."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate the hardware ridge point from peak compute throughput and memory bandwidth.", "You can now apply the roofline equation to compute a modeled upper bound and identify whether the bandwidth or compute ceiling is lower.", "You can now analyze why rising compute-to-bandwidth ratios push newer hardware ridge points upward and increase the need for reuse-aware kernel design.", "You can now evaluate why increasing peak compute alone does not raise the simple roofline bound for a memory-bound kernel."]}], "id": "v1-11-06-performance-bounds", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-07-hardware-mapping": {"title": "Hardware Mapping", "summary": "Compare spatial mapping and temporal mapping dimensions in AI hardware acceleration.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Hardware Mapping", "Placement and allocation", "Combinatorial complexity", "Dataflow optimization", "Stationary dataflows", "Tiling", "Memory-efficient tensor layouts", "Kernel fusion on the accelerator", "Applying mapping strategies to neural networks"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Hardware Mapping** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Compare spatial mapping and temporal mapping dimensions in AI hardware acceleration\n- Compare how weight-stationary and output-stationary dataflows move operands and why different reuse patterns favor each one\n- Justify the primary objective of tiling and memory hierarchy mapping\n- Compare the memory layout efficiency of Channels-Last (NHWC) versus Channels-First (NCHW) for Tensor Cores\n- Explain the performance benefits and hardware constraints of vertical kernel fusion"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Hardware Mapping", "md": "Consider one of ResNet-50's \\$3×3 convolutions running on an accelerator tile. The mathematical operation is fixed, but the execution plan is not. One schedule can keep a filter in local registers while many output pixels stream past it; another can advance pixel by pixel and reload the same filter values repeatedly from a slower memory tier. Both schedules compute the same tensor. Only one turns the reuse in the convolution into real bandwidth savings."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Placement and allocation\n- Combinatorial complexity\n- Dataflow optimization\n- Stationary dataflows\n- Tiling\n- Memory-efficient tensor layouts\n- Kernel fusion on the accelerator\n- Applying mapping strategies to neural networks\n\nPay attention to: Spatial mapping (PE/SM assignment) vs Temporal mapping (loop scheduling); Placement and resource allocation across memory hierarchy levels; Combinatorial complexity of loop transformations and tile selection."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Contrast the data movement mechanics and primary use cases of Weight-Stationary (WS) and Output-Stationary (OS) systolic array dataflows.", "md": "In a weight-stationary dataflow, weights are kept local in processing elements while activations stream past them and partial sums move through the array, so the mapping favors cases where one weight tile is reused many times before eviction. In an output-stationary dataflow, partial sums stay local until all contributions arrive, reducing repeated write-back of intermediates while weights and activations stream. A CNN layer with strong filter reuse is a common WS candidate, whereas a deep accumulation pattern can make OS attractive because it preserves the running output locally. The practical consequence is that neither dataflow is universally best; the right choice depends on reuse, buffer capacity, and tensor shape."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In neural network hardware mapping, what distinguishes a spatial mapping decision from a temporal mapping decision?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Spatial mapping decides how tensor dimensions and parallel operations are partitioned and assigned across physical execution resources (such as array PEs, GPU SMs, or SIMD vector lanes) to execute concurrently in space. Temporal mapping decides the chronological schedule, loop ordering, and time steps in which operations and tile iterations execute on those assigned physical units over time. The compiler IR vs runtime launch distinction confuses compilation phases with mapping dimensions. The precision vs memory allocation choice describes quantization and memory planning. The layer-specific choice is incorrect as both mapping types apply to all neural network operations.", "type": "single", "options": ["Spatial mapping refers to compiling graph IR, whereas temporal mapping refers to runtime CUDA kernel launches", "Spatial mapping determines precision formats (FP16 vs INT8), whereas temporal mapping determines memory allocation sizes", "Spatial mapping assigns computational tasks to specific physical execution units (e.g., PEs or SMs) simultaneously in parallel, whereas temporal mapping determines the execution ordering and loop scheduling over time on those units", "Spatial mapping operates only on convolutional layers, whereas temporal mapping operates only on transformer attention layers"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: compare spatial mapping and temporal mapping dimensions in AI hardware acceleration."]}, {"q": "When mapping a tensor computation to a multi-level memory hierarchy, what is the primary objective optimized by tiling, whether through implicit cache blocking or explicit scratchpad staging?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The primary goal of tiling, whether caches capture the reuse implicitly or the kernel stages tiles explicitly, is to maximize data reuse in the highest levels of the memory hierarchy (registers and on-chip SRAM/shared memory), ensuring that each byte fetched from power-hungry, high-latency DRAM is reused as many times as possible before eviction, thereby minimizing DRAM bandwidth demand and maximizing compute throughput. Maximizing writes to host memory degrades performance. Executing different instructions causes severe warp divergence. Converting matrix operations to scalars eliminates vector and tensor unit acceleration.", "type": "single", "options": ["Maximizing the total number of intermediate tensors written to host DDR memory", "Maximizing data reuse in the fastest, closest memory tiers (registers and SRAM) to minimize traffic to slower, energy-expensive DRAM", "Ensuring every warp thread executes different instruction streams simultaneously", "Converting all 2D matrix multiplications into 1D scalar operations"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: justify the primary objective of tiling and memory hierarchy mapping."]}, {"q": "Why do modern deep learning libraries (e.g., cuDNN, TensorRT) strongly prefer the Channels-Last (NHWC) tensor layout over Channels-First (NCHW) when executing convolutions on NVIDIA Tensor Cores?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. In NHWC layout, the channel dimension $C$ is the fastest-varying (innermost) dimension, placing all channel features for a specific spatial coordinate $(n, h, w)$ contiguously in memory. Tensor Cores execute matrix operations on contiguous vectors of channels (e.g., 8 or 16 channels per memory vector load), enabling coalesced memory access and direct loading into matrix unit registers without expensive transpose or gather operations. The 1D flattening, big-endian, and parameter sharing choices are technically incorrect.", "type": "single", "options": ["NHWC eliminates the need for spatial convolutions by flattening images into 1D vectors", "NCHW requires floating-point numbers to be stored in big-endian byte order", "NHWC reduces model parameter count by sharing channel weights across batches", "NHWC places channel values for a given spatial location in contiguous memory, aligning with the packed vector/matrix multiply requirements of Tensor Cores"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: compare the memory layout efficiency of Channels-Last (NHWC) versus Channels-First (NCHW) for Tensor Cores."]}, {"q": "Row-Stationary (RS) dataflow, as implemented in architectures like Eyeriss, keeps only the final output activation stationary in registers while streaming 2D convolutional filter rows and input rows from DRAM on every clock cycle.", "explanation": "False. Row-Stationary dataflow keeps 1D rows of convolutional weights stationary in PE registers, slides 1D rows of input activations through the PEs, and accumulates 1D rows of partial sums locally across a 2D array of PEs, maximizing 2D convolution spatial reuse across all three tensor components simultaneously.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the operational mechanics of Row-Stationary dataflow in convolution accelerators."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Explain the mechanism of vertical kernel fusion (e.g., fusing `Conv2D` $\\to$ `BatchNorm` $\\to$ `ReLU`), and identify both its performance benefit and its primary architectural constraint.", "md": "Vertical kernel fusion combines a sequence of producer-consumer operations into a single GPU kernel, passing intermediate activation values directly through fast on-chip registers or shared memory instead of writing them out to and re-reading them from global DRAM (HBM). This dramatically reduces memory traffic and kernel launch overhead. Its primary architectural constraint is register pressure: storing intermediate variables and fusing complex pipelines increases register usage per thread, which can reduce warp occupancy and limit available parallelism on the SM."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now compare spatial mapping and temporal mapping dimensions in AI hardware acceleration.", "You can now compare how weight-stationary and output-stationary dataflows move operands and why different reuse patterns favor each one.", "You can now justify the primary objective of tiling and memory hierarchy mapping.", "You can now compare the memory layout efficiency of Channels-Last (NHWC) versus Channels-First (NCHW) for Tensor Cores."]}], "id": "v1-11-07-hardware-mapping", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-08-compilers-and-runtimes": {"title": "Compilers and Runtimes", "summary": "Explain the role of tensor lifetime analysis in static memory planning.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Compilers and Runtimes", "ML compiler design", "ML compilation pipeline", "Runtime support"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Compilers and Runtimes** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain the role of tensor lifetime analysis in static memory planning\n- Sequence the illustrative end-to-end stages of an ML compiler from graph ingestion through target code generation\n- Compare compiler-driven auto-tuning search with the expert-tuned implementation strategy emphasized by vendor libraries\n- Explain how asynchronous queues and device events preserve dependencies without blocking the host after each kernel launch, enabling overlap in repeated accelerator workflows\n- Analyze the non-blocking execution model of asynchronous GPU runtime streams"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Compilers and Runtimes", "md": "The roofline gives each ResNet-50 layer a ceiling, and hardware mapping showed which schedules can approach it, but neither reaches the silicon by itself. Every layer needs a tiling, a stationary operand, a tensor layout, a precision for the activations it reads and writes, and a fusion boundary. The resulting plan must then run on a device whose input shapes, free memory, and clock rate change while it serves. Across a whole model these choices number in the thousands, which is why compilers and runtimes, not engineers, make them."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- ML compiler design\n- ML compilation pipeline\n- Runtime support\n\nPay attention to: ML compilation pipeline: High-Level IR -> Low-Level Loop IR -> Code Generation; Graph optimizations: Operator fusion, constant folding, dead code elimination; Memory planning and buffer reuse via tensor lifetime analysis; Kernel selection: Template libraries vs auto-tuning (TVM, Ansor, Triton)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain how host command queues, asynchronous streams, and device events preserve execution dependencies without forcing the host to block after each kernel launch, and how that permits overlap in repeated training or inference iterations on accelerators.", "md": "The host can enqueue kernel launches and memory copies onto asynchronous streams so dependencies are enforced by device events and queue ordering rather than by making the CPU wait for each completion. For a repeated workflow, this allows copies and kernels to overlap when dependencies permit, while the host continues launching work instead of synchronizing after every step. The practical implication is that repeated execution is less likely to be dominated by serialized host-side waiting, especially when many short operations would otherwise stall on frequent CPU synchronization."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "What is the primary purpose of lifetime analysis in an ML compiler's static memory planner?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Lifetime analysis tracks when each intermediate tensor is created (produced) and last referenced (consumed) during graph execution. By identifying tensors whose lifetimes do not overlap, the compiler's static memory planner can assign them to the same physical memory addresses (buffer reuse/aliasing), significantly reducing the model's peak runtime memory footprint and avoiding dynamic memory allocation overhead. The physical degradation choice confuses tensor lifecycle with semiconductor physics. The training convergence choice describes algorithmic optimization rather than compiler memory management. The instruction stream choice describes scheduling.", "type": "single", "options": ["To calculate the physical degradation and failure rate of HBM memory cells over time", "To determine the precise intervals during which each intermediate activation tensor is needed, allowing disjoint tensors to share the same physical memory buffer", "To predict the number of training epochs required for a neural network to converge", "To prevent the compiler from generating out-of-order instruction streams"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: explain the role of tensor lifetime analysis in static memory planning."]}, {"q": "Order the following compilation stages for the illustrative compiler workflow. In this workflow, static memory planning uses the tuned lowered schedule, so it comes after lowering and auto-tuning:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (3) Front-End Ingestion: Parse framework model into High-Level Graph IR, (2) High-Level Graph Optimization: Perform operator fusion, constant folding, and dead code elimination on the computation graph, (4) Low-Level IR & Auto-Tuning: Lower fused operators to loop-level IR and optimize tile sizes, thread bindings, and unroll factors, (5) Static Memory Planning: Analyze tensor lifetimes and allocate shared physical buffers, (1) Target Code Generation: Emit hardware-specific target code. In this illustrative workflow, the compiler first needs a graph representation before it can optimize it, then lowers optimized operators into loop-level forms where schedule choices can be tuned. Static memory planning then consumes that tuned lowered schedule to plan memory over the resulting execution, and the compiler finally emits target-specific code. Some real systems iterate among these stages or place memory planning differently, but for this workflow code generation cannot happen before the compiler has settled the lowered execution plan it intends to emit.", "type": "order", "items": ["High-Level Graph Optimization: Perform operator fusion, constant folding, and dead code elimination on the computation graph", "Low-Level IR & Auto-Tuning: Lower fused operators to loop-level IR and optimize tile sizes, thread bindings, and unroll factors", "Static Memory Planning: Analyze tensor lifetimes and allocate shared physical buffers"], "hints": ["Think about: sequence the illustrative end-to-end stages of an ML compiler from graph ingestion through target code generation."]}, {"q": "How does an auto-tuning ML compiler (such as TVM/Ansor) differ from a traditional handwritten library approach (such as cuDNN) for kernel selection?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Auto-tuning compiler systems search parameterized spaces of schedules such as tiling, unrolling, and thread mapping, then rank or measure candidates to produce kernels specialized to the target and shape. Handwritten libraries also use parameters, heuristics, and benchmarking tactics, but their emphasis is expert-designed implementations rather than open-ended schedule search by the compiler. The host-CPU claim is false, the infinite-runtime-search claim misstates how libraries operate, and 1-bit quantization is unrelated.", "type": "single", "options": ["Auto-tuning compilers execute code only on the host CPU, whereas handwritten libraries run on GPUs", "Handwritten libraries search an infinite combinatorial loop space at runtime, whereas auto-tuning compilers use static heuristics", "Auto-tuning compilers explore large parameterized search spaces of loop transformations and tile sizes using cost models or measurements to generate custom kernels, whereas handwritten libraries emphasize expert-tuned implementations and heuristics", "Auto-tuning compilers require all tensors to be quantized to 1-bit integers"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: compare compiler-driven auto-tuning search with the expert-tuned implementation strategy emphasized by vendor libraries."]}, {"q": "In an asynchronous GPU runtime model, when a Python script executes `y = torch.matmul(a, b)`, the CPU thread blocks and waits until the GPU hardware finishes computing the matrix multiplication before executing the next line of Python code.", "explanation": "False. GPU runtime calls are asynchronous: `torch.matmul` enqueues the kernel execution command onto a CUDA stream queue in the driver and immediately returns control to the CPU thread. The CPU continues executing subsequent Python instructions while the GPU executes the kernel in the background, blocking only when synchronization is explicitly requested (e.g., via `torch.cuda.synchronize()` or copying data back to CPU host memory).", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the non-blocking execution model of asynchronous GPU runtime streams."]}, {"q": "An inference serving system processes dynamic batch sizes ranging from a batch of 1 to 32 concurrent decode requests, each contributing one token at the current step. Why does dynamic batching pose a significant challenge to runtime kernel selection and hardware efficiency?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Changing batch size changes shape, available reuse, and parallel work, so a kernel configuration that is effective at one batch can be inefficient at another. Small batches may emphasize low-latency execution and limited reuse, while larger batches can expose more parallelism and justify different tiling or tactic choices. The precision-switching claim is invented, Tensor Cores are not limited to powers of two in that way, and DRAM bank power-down is not the core runtime-kernel-selection issue described here.", "type": "single", "options": ["Optimal tile sizes, thread block configurations, and memory-reuse opportunities change across batch sizes, making a single static kernel inefficient across all shapes", "Dynamic batching forces the GPU to switch from FP16 to FP64 precision for even batch sizes", "Tensor Cores cannot execute matrix multiplication when batch dimensions are not powers of two", "The GPU memory controller must physically power down DRAM banks when batch size decreases"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why dynamic batch sizes make a single static kernel configuration inefficient across inference requests."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain the role of tensor lifetime analysis in static memory planning.", "You can now sequence the illustrative end-to-end stages of an ML compiler from graph ingestion through target code generation.", "You can now compare compiler-driven auto-tuning search with the expert-tuned implementation strategy emphasized by vendor libraries.", "You can now explain how asynchronous queues and device events preserve dependencies without blocking the host after each kernel launch, enabling overlap in repeated accelerator workflows."]}], "id": "v1-11-08-compilers-and-runtimes", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-09-beyond-one-accelerator": {"title": "Beyond One Accelerator", "summary": "Justify the transition from monolithic dies to chiplet-based accelerator architectures.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Beyond One Accelerator"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Beyond One Accelerator** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Justify the transition from monolithic dies to chiplet-based accelerator architectures\n- Analyze how widening communication boundaries from package to node to cluster changes the cost of scaling neural network workloads\n- Explain how wafer-scale integration reduces some off-chip communication bottlenecks while leaving routing, thermal, and fault-tolerance constraints in place\n- Identify why moving from intra-node to inter-node communication makes distributed collectives more costly"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Beyond One Accelerator", "md": "A single accelerator holds the chapter's ResNet-50 workload and GPT-2 XL decoding comfortably in on-package memory, but larger workloads outgrow one device either because their arithmetic would take too long on it or because their weights and runtime state do not fit in its HBM. Multi-chip scaling still depends on an efficient per-device dataflow, since each accelerator must feed its own execution units, but communication adds further boundaries to the memory hierarchy."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: Monolithic reticle limits and the shift to Multi-Chip Modules (MCM) / chiplets; Inter-chip bandwidth drops and NUMA effects across package boundaries; Wafer-scale integration vs board-level interconnects (NVLink, NVSwitch); Scale-out network bandwidth constraints compared to intra-node interconnects."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain how communication boundaries change performance as a neural network workload scales from chiplets within a package to multiple accelerators within a node and then across nodes.", "md": "As scaling moves outward from package-level chiplets to multiple accelerators in a node and then to separate nodes, communication generally crosses slower and more expensive boundaries. A tensor read that stays local to one chip or package can become more costly when it must traverse an intra-node fabric, and cost rises again when collectives extend across the network. For example, a partitioning that is acceptable within one node may become communication-heavy once activations or gradients must repeatedly cross the internode boundary. The practical consequence is that topology, locality, and collective patterns matter: performance depends not just on how much compute is added, but on which communication boundary the workload is forced to cross repeatedly."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-09-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why have accelerator architectures increasingly shifted from monolithic single-die designs toward Multi-Chip Module (MCM) and chiplet architectures?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Monolithic silicon dies are physically bounded by optical lithography reticle limits (typically $\\approx 858\\text{ mm}^2$). Furthermore, manufacturing defect density causes wafer yield to drop exponentially as die area approaches the reticle limit, making large monolithic chips prohibitively expensive. Chiplet/MCM architectures break the system into smaller, high-yield dies interconnected via high-density silicon bridges or interposers, enabling much larger aggregate compute and memory capacity. The elimination of foundries is absurd; HBM is supported on monolithic dies (e.g., A100/H100); physical constants cannot be exceeded.", "type": "single", "options": ["Chiplets eliminate all need for semiconductor fabrication foundries", "Monolithic dies are constrained by the physical lithography reticle limit ($\\approx 858\\text{ mm}^2$) and suffer exponential yield loss as die size increases", "Monolithic dies cannot support high-bandwidth memory (HBM) interfaces", "Chiplets allow electrical signals to travel faster than the speed of light"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: justify the transition from monolithic dies to chiplet-based accelerator architectures."]}, {"q": "What is the primary bottleneck introduced when scaling out from accelerators within one server to accelerators across separate servers?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Scaling across servers pushes communication onto a narrower, higher-latency network boundary than the local accelerator fabric, so collectives such as all-reduce or all-to-all become more expensive and more likely to limit scaling. The floating-point-format claim is false, the CPU-emulation claim invents a mechanism that does not exist, and the synchronization claim gets the direction wrong because distributed training still has to exchange information across ranks.", "type": "single", "options": ["Inter-node network adapters cannot transmit FP16 or BF16 floating-point values", "Inter-node communication requires GPUs to switch to single-threaded CPU emulation mode", "The communication boundary moves onto a much narrower and higher-latency network path, making collective communication more expensive", "Inter-node scaling eliminates the need for gradient synchronization in distributed training"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: identify why moving from intra-node to inter-node communication makes distributed collectives more costly."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Describe the architectural rationale behind a wafer-scale accelerator and explain which multi-chip bottlenecks it reduces and which constraints remain.", "md": "A wafer-scale design keeps much more computation and communication on one physical substrate instead of splitting it across many separately packaged chips, so it can reduce external communication boundaries that would otherwise require off-chip links and package crossings. That can lower some latency and increase local bandwidth for workloads that would have exchanged data across chip boundaries in a conventional multi-chip system. However, it does not make the entire wafer a uniform zero-cost machine: distance, routing capacity, thermal limits, fault tolerance, and manufacturing constraints still shape which communication patterns remain efficient. The practical implication is that wafer scale reduces some multi-chip bottlenecks, but it does not eliminate locality or systems-design constraints."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now justify the transition from monolithic dies to chiplet-based accelerator architectures.", "You can now analyze how widening communication boundaries from package to node to cluster changes the cost of scaling neural network workloads.", "You can now explain how wafer-scale integration reduces some off-chip communication bottlenecks while leaving routing, thermal, and fault-tolerance constraints in place.", "You can now identify why moving from intra-node to inter-node communication makes distributed collectives more costly."]}], "id": "v1-11-09-beyond-one-accelerator", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-10-heterogeneous-soc-design": {"title": "Heterogeneous SoC Design", "summary": "Classify which SoC engine is typically most energy-efficient for a supported INT8 convolution workload.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Heterogeneous SoC Design", "Mobile SoC architecture evolution", "Strategies for dynamic workload distribution", "Power and thermal management", "Software stack challenges"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Heterogeneous SoC Design** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Classify which SoC engine is typically most energy-efficient for a supported INT8 convolution workload\n- Explain why automotive SoCs prioritize isolation and worst-case deadline guarantees over average-case throughput\n- Evaluate the memory movement and cache coherency trade-offs of unified SoC memory\n- Analyze the impact of thermal throttling and DVFS on sustained edge AI inference"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Heterogeneous SoC Design", "md": "Edge, mobile, and automotive deployments operate under strict physical constraints: sealed enclosures without active fans limit thermal dissipation to a few watts, while battery capacity caps total energy consumption. To deliver machine learning capabilities within these boundaries, heterogeneous system-on-chip (SoC) architectures integrate general-purpose CPU cores, GPU shader arrays, digital signal processors (DSPs), and specialized neural processing units (NPUs) onto a single silicon die sharing a unified memory subsystem."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Mobile SoC architecture evolution\n- Strategies for dynamic workload distribution\n- Power and thermal management\n- Software stack challenges\n\nPay attention to: Mobile SoC architecture: CPU, GPU, NPU/DSP, and unified LPDDR memory; Dynamic workload distribution based on latency, power, and throughput; Power and thermal management: DVFS, thermal throttling, and dark silicon; Automotive heterogeneous AI: ISO 26262 functional safety, lockstep redundancy, deterministic latency."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: In automotive autonomous-driving SoCs, explain why mixed-criticality isolation, bandwidth reservations, and worst-case deadlines matter even when they can reduce average throughput.", "md": "Automotive platforms run safety-critical perception and control alongside less critical workloads, so the system must guarantee that urgent tasks still meet their deadlines under contention rather than only performing well on average. Isolation mechanisms and bandwidth reservations prevent a noncritical workload from monopolizing shared memory or compute resources needed by a real-time pipeline. For example, reserving memory bandwidth for a perception path may lower average accelerator utilization, but it protects the worst-case execution time that braking or steering logic depends on. The practical consequence is that the design target is deadline reliability under mixed-criticality load, not maximum average throughput alone."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-10-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "On a modern mobile heterogeneous System-on-Chip (SoC) featuring big.LITTLE CPUs, a mobile GPU, and a dedicated NPU, which compute engine is typically the most energy-efficient choice for a supported INT8 convolution workload?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. For a supported INT8 convolution path, the NPU is designed specifically to execute quantized tensor operations with better energy efficiency than a general-purpose CPU or GPU path. The measured pipeline energy still depends on fallback operators, data movement, and thermal behavior, so specialization does not by itself determine the whole-system result. The big CPU core is less specialized for this workload, and the memory controller and hypervisor are not the units that execute the convolution itself.", "type": "single", "options": ["The high-performance 'big' CPU core running single-threaded FP32 instructions", "The out-of-order system memory controller", "The dedicated Neural Processing Unit (NPU) optimized for quantized INT8 tensor operations", "The host operating system virtualization hypervisor"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: classify which SoC engine is typically most energy-efficient for a supported INT8 convolution workload."]}, {"q": "In mobile SoCs with unified system memory (LPDDR), sharing physical RAM between the CPU, GPU, and NPU eliminates all data movement overhead between heterogeneous processors.", "explanation": "False. While unified memory eliminates physical PCIe bus transfers and duplicate memory copies across discrete devices, data must still be moved across the shared on-chip interconnect and through different cache hierarchy domains. Furthermore, cache coherency protocols, differing tensor layout requirements (e.g., NCHW vs NHWC), and memory bus contention between competing SoC engines still incur significant latency and energy overhead.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the memory movement and cache coherency trade-offs of unified SoC memory."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Describe how Dynamic Voltage and Frequency Scaling (DVFS) and thermal throttling constrain sustained AI inference performance on edge and mobile devices.", "md": "Mobile SoCs operate within a tight thermal design power (TDP) envelope (typically \\$3--5 W) with passive cooling. When continuous AI workloads generate sustained heat, the thermal management system triggers DVFS to lower operating voltage and clock frequencies, preventing device overheating. This thermal throttling causes execution time to degrade over time, meaning peak burst performance cannot be sustained for continuous video or audio streaming workloads without proactive power-budget scheduling."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now classify which SoC engine is typically most energy-efficient for a supported INT8 convolution workload.", "You can now explain why automotive SoCs prioritize isolation and worst-case deadline guarantees over average-case throughput.", "You can now evaluate the memory movement and cache coherency trade-offs of unified SoC memory.", "You can now analyze the impact of thermal throttling and DVFS on sustained edge AI inference."]}], "id": "v1-11-10-heterogeneous-soc-design", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-11-choosing-an-accelerator": {"title": "Choosing an Accelerator", "summary": "Match each lighthouse workload to the accelerator resource it exhausts first.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Choosing an Accelerator", "Processor configurations across deployment targets", "Cost-performance analysis", "Energy per request"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Choosing an Accelerator** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Match each lighthouse workload to the accelerator resource it exhausts first\n- Compare embodied carbon and operational carbon in AI hardware lifecycle analysis\n- Explain how higher useful utilization can lower embodied carbon per result without guaranteeing lower total emissions"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Choosing an Accelerator", "md": "Every section of this chapter ends in the same question asked at a different level: which machine should run a given workload. The question cannot be answered from a datasheet, because the limiting resource belongs to the workload on a particular machine rather than to the machine alone. The book's five lighthouses make the point on a single reference machine. Their bytes and operations differ by orders of magnitude, and each one exhausts a different resource of an H100 first (a later section)."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Processor configurations across deployment targets\n- Cost-performance analysis\n- Energy per request\n\nPay attention to: Embodied carbon vs operational carbon in semiconductor manufacturing; Performance per watt (TFLOPs/W, TOPS/W) as a sustainability metric; Datacenter Power Usage Effectiveness (PUE) and cooling efficiency; Hardware utilization and amortizing manufacturing emissions."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why maximizing useful accelerator utilization can reduce the amortized carbon footprint per completed AI workload, while not automatically reducing total carbon emissions.", "md": "When a device spends more of its lifetime doing useful completed work, the embodied impact of manufacturing that hardware is spread across more results, so the allocated embodied carbon per trained model or served workload can fall. For example, if the same accelerator fleet completes more validated training runs or inferences over its service life, each result carries a smaller share of the hardware's fixed manufacturing burden. The practical implication is that higher useful utilization can improve carbon efficiency per result, but it does not automatically lower total emissions because total power draw, workload quality, and the amount of work done can all rise at the same time."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-11-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "On a single H100, which pairing of lighthouse workload and the resource it exhausts first matches the chapter's lighthouse memory-and-compute comparison?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Batched ResNet-50 reuses each weight across many images and positions, so arithmetic throughput sets the limit once weights are amortized. Batch-one GPT-2 XL decode rereads every weight for each generated token at about one operation per byte, so HBM bandwidth is the bottleneck. DLRM's embedding tables exceed one device's memory, so capacity runs out before either arithmetic or bandwidth. The other options assign each lighthouse a resource it does not exhaust first, and the last ignores that the limiting resource belongs to the workload on the machine rather than to the machine alone.", "type": "single", "options": ["ResNet-50: memory capacity; GPT-2 XL decode: arithmetic throughput; DLRM: memory bandwidth", "ResNet-50: arithmetic throughput once batching amortizes its weights; GPT-2 XL decode: HBM bandwidth; DLRM: memory capacity", "ResNet-50: HBM bandwidth; GPT-2 XL decode: memory capacity; DLRM: arithmetic throughput", "All three are limited by arithmetic throughput, because the H100's tensor peak is the limiting resource for every workload"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: match each lighthouse workload to the accelerator resource it exhausts first."]}, {"q": "In the lifecycle carbon assessment of advanced deep learning accelerators, what constitutes 'embodied carbon'?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Embodied carbon refers to the total greenhouse gas emissions generated throughout the supply chain and manufacturing lifecycle of the hardware before it ever runs a workload, including silicon ingot purification, advanced extreme ultraviolet (EUV) lithography, cleanroom fabrication, multi-chip packaging, assembly, and transportation. Operational carbon refers to emissions resulting from electricity consumed during active operation and cooling of the hardware. Carbon credits are financial offsets, not physical emissions.", "type": "single", "options": ["The electrical energy consumed by the GPU during model forward and backward passes", "The carbon emitted by datacenter air conditioning units during peak summer load", "The carbon credits purchased by cloud providers to offset datacenter energy usage", "The greenhouse gas emissions generated during raw material extraction, semiconductor silicon manufacturing, packaging, and hardware transportation"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: compare embodied carbon and operational carbon in AI hardware lifecycle analysis."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now match each lighthouse workload to the accelerator resource it exhausts first.", "You can now compare embodied carbon and operational carbon in AI hardware lifecycle analysis.", "You can now explain how higher useful utilization can lower embodied carbon per result without guaranteeing lower total emissions."]}], "id": "v1-11-11-choosing-an-accelerator", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-12-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Explain why unstructured sparsity often fails to accelerate inference on dense accelerator paths.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-11", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Fallacies and Pitfalls** in *Hardware Acceleration* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain why unstructured sparsity often fails to accelerate inference on dense accelerator paths\n- Analyze the performance pitfalls of kernel launch and data transfer overhead in micro-offloading\n- Apply a given diagnostic procedure that starts with end-to-end profiling, checks distributed communication, then uses kernel analysis to choose the right intervention\n- Evaluate why peak TFLOP/s alone cannot predict end-to-end workload speedup"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Hardware Acceleration, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "Hardware acceleration often defies intuition because headline specifications mask workload-specific bottlenecks. The fallacies and pitfalls here capture selection, scaling, and optimization errors that leave expensive compute resources underused. Sound system evaluation follows one diagnostic habit instead: characterize the workload arithmetic intensity, identify the physical bottleneck metric, match execution to the appropriate datapath, and account for interconnect, scaling, and portability costs."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Summary\n\nPay attention to: Fallacy of peak FLOPS as a predictor of real-world speedup; Pitfalls of unstructured sparsity on dense hardware architectures; Micro-offloading latency overhead and small tensor kernel launch penalties; Systematic diagnostic workflows for identifying hardware bottlenecks."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Describe the pitfall of 'micro-offloading' small tensor operations from CPU to GPU, and explain why a sequence of scalar operations can run slower on an accelerator than on the host CPU.", "md": "Offloading small tensor operations incurs PCIe host-to-device data transfer latency, host driver scheduling overhead, and kernel launch latency ($\\approx 5\\text{--}10\\,\\mu\\text{s}$ per kernel). When a tensor contains only a few dozen or hundred elements, computation time is sub-microsecond, meaning fixed transfer and launch overhead completely dominates execution time. Furthermore, small tensors cannot provide enough parallel work to saturate thousands of GPU cores, making scalar or vector execution on low-latency CPU caches much faster."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-12-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A team prunes \\$70\\% of the weights in a large language model using unstructured magnitude pruning, setting those weights to zero. However, when executing the pruned model on standard GPU dense Tensor Cores, inference latency is identical to or slower than the unpruned baseline. What is the primary cause of this pitfall?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Standard dense Tensor Core paths still operate on dense matrix shapes, so setting many entries to zero does not by itself cause the hardware to skip those multiplies. If the model is converted to a general sparse format, metadata and irregular gathers can offset or erase the theoretical arithmetic reduction. The infinite-loop, thermal-downclock, and page-fault explanations invent mechanisms unrelated to why dense hardware often fails to speed up unstructured sparsity.", "type": "single", "options": ["Standard dense hardware cannot skip arbitrary zero elements efficiently, and compact sparse formats add metadata and irregular access overhead", "Floating-point units automatically convert zero values into infinite loops", "Unstructured pruning forces the GPU driver to downclock memory bandwidth to prevent overheating", "The operating system kernel intercepts every zero multiplication and raises a hardware page fault"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: explain why unstructured sparsity often fails to accelerate inference on dense accelerator paths."]}, {"q": "For the given diagnostic procedure for an underperforming training workload on an already distributed accelerator cluster—broad trace first, then communication check, then exposed-kernel analysis, then intervention—place the following steps in that sequence:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) End-to-End Profiling: Identify data loading stalls, host-device synchronization, and the full critical path, (4) Multi-Device Communication Profiling: Inspect collective communication delays and interconnect saturation, (1) Roofline & Hardware Counter Analysis: Determine whether exposed kernels are compute-bound, memory-bandwidth-bound, or latency-bound, (3) Targeted Optimization: Apply the intervention that matches the identified critical bottleneck. This answer preserves the given diagnostic procedure: first trace the full path, then check communication, then inspect exposed kernels, and only then choose the intervention. It is not a universal order required in every debugging case: if the broad trace already proves the root cause is host-side or communication-side, the intervention can be chosen without unnecessary deep kernel analysis. The point is to follow the stated procedure and avoid prematurely tuning a kernel that is not actually the limiting step.", "type": "order", "items": ["End-to-End Profiling: Identify data loading stalls, host-device synchronization, and the full critical path", "Multi-Device Communication Profiling: Inspect collective communication delays and interconnect saturation", "Roofline & Hardware Counter Analysis: Determine whether exposed kernels are compute-bound, memory-bandwidth-bound, or latency-bound", "Targeted Optimization: Apply the intervention that matches the identified critical bottleneck"], "hints": ["Think about: apply a given diagnostic procedure that starts with end-to-end profiling, checks distributed communication, then uses kernel analysis to choose the right intervention."]}, {"q": "Which of the following statements represents a classic fallacy regarding peak accelerator specifications?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Treating a \\$2\\times$ increase in peak TFLOP/s as an automatic \\$2\\times$ workload speedup ignores memory-bandwidth limits, host and launch overheads, unsupported operators, and other bottlenecks that often dominate real execution. The higher-bandwidth statement is a conditional architectural fact rather than a fallacy, and the fusion and warp-divergence statements describe real mechanisms discussed in the chapter.", "type": "single", "options": ["Higher memory bandwidth can raise the bandwidth ceiling for some memory-bound workloads", "An accelerator with \\$2\\times$ higher peak theoretical TFLOP/s will automatically deliver a \\$2\\times$ speedup on any neural network workload", "Kernel fusion can improve arithmetic intensity by reducing global memory traffic", "Warp divergence reduces the execution efficiency of SIMT processor lanes"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate why peak TFLOP/s alone cannot predict end-to-end workload speedup."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain why unstructured sparsity often fails to accelerate inference on dense accelerator paths.", "You can now analyze the performance pitfalls of kernel launch and data transfer overhead in micro-offloading.", "You can now apply a given diagnostic procedure that starts with end-to-end profiling, checks distributed communication, then uses kernel analysis to choose the right intervention.", "You can now evaluate why peak TFLOP/s alone cannot predict end-to-end workload speedup."]}], "id": "v1-11-12-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v1-11", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-11-99-test": {"title": "Test: Hardware Acceleration", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-11-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"q": "Why does High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) achieve significantly higher bandwidth (e.g., $>2\\text{ TB/s}$) than traditional GDDR6X memory (e.g., $\\approx 760\\text{ GB/s}$) while maintaining comparable or lower power per bit?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. HBM achieves ultra-high bandwidth by vertically stacking DRAM dies with Through-Silicon Vias (TSVs) and interfacing with the accelerator through a silicon interposer with extremely wide memory buses (1024 bits per stack vs. 32/64 bits for standard GDDR channels). Because physical wire distances over the silicon interposer are short and the bus is wide, HBM can run at lower pin clock frequencies, significantly reducing energy per bit ($pJ/\\text{bit}$) compared to driving high-frequency GDDR signals over long PCB traces. The higher clock frequency choice is incorrect because GDDR6X actually runs at higher pin clock frequencies than HBM. The optical photonics and error-correction elimination choices are physically false.", "type": "single", "options": ["HBM operates at a \\$10× higher clock frequency than GDDR6X on standard PCB traces", "HBM uses optical photonic signaling to transmit data across the motherboard", "HBM eliminates all error correction codes and row buffer precharge cycles", "HBM vertically stacks DRAM dies using Through-Silicon Vias (TSVs) and connects to the GPU via a wide 1024-bit per stack silicon interposer bus at lower clock speeds"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain the architectural design differences and physical advantages of HBM over GDDR."]}, {"q": "The AI Memory Wall refers solely to the limited physical capacity (GBs) of GPU DRAM, meaning that if an accelerator has sufficient gigabytes to store model weights, memory bandwidth will never bottleneck execution.", "explanation": "False. In this section, the AI memory wall is the gap between arithmetic throughput and memory bandwidth. Capacity is a separate feasibility constraint: a model can fit in memory and still run slowly if low-reuse kernels, such as LayerNorm or token-by-token decoding, are limited by bytes delivered per second rather than by available gigabytes.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish memory-capacity limits from bandwidth bottlenecks in the AI memory wall."]}, {"q": "In the Roofline model, the transition point on the horizontal axis where the memory-bandwidth ceiling intersects the peak-compute ceiling is known as the hardware ____ point.", "explanation": "ridge. The ridge point ($I_{\\text{ridge}} = R_{\\text{peak}} / \\text{BW}$) defines the minimum arithmetic intensity required for an algorithm to potentially reach peak hardware computational throughput.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["ridge"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the significance of the ridge point in roofline performance modeling."]}, {"q": "What is the primary numerical difference between FP16 and BF16 as presented in this section?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Both FP16 and BF16 are 16-bit floating-point formats, but BF16 keeps an 8-bit exponent like FP32, giving it a wider dynamic range, while FP16 devotes more bits to the stored fraction and therefore offers finer precision within a narrower range. The lower-byte-count claim is false because both use 16 bits, the integer claim is wrong because both are floating point, and the underflow claim is too strong because numerical behavior depends on the workload and execution path.", "type": "single", "options": ["FP16 uses fewer total bits than BF16, so it always transfers less data", "BF16 has a wider exponent range, while FP16 has more fraction precision within the same 16-bit width", "FP16 is an integer format, whereas BF16 is floating point", "BF16 always prevents gradient underflow, whereas FP16 cannot be used for training"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: compare FP16 and BF16 in terms of dynamic range versus fraction precision using the format properties introduced in the section."]}, {"q": "A team prunes \\$70\\% of the weights in a large language model using unstructured magnitude pruning, setting those weights to zero. However, when executing the pruned model on standard GPU dense Tensor Cores, inference latency is identical to or slower than the unpruned baseline. What is the primary cause of this pitfall?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Standard dense Tensor Core paths still operate on dense matrix shapes, so setting many entries to zero does not by itself cause the hardware to skip those multiplies. If the model is converted to a general sparse format, metadata and irregular gathers can offset or erase the theoretical arithmetic reduction. The infinite-loop, thermal-downclock, and page-fault explanations invent mechanisms unrelated to why dense hardware often fails to speed up unstructured sparsity.", "type": "single", "options": ["Standard dense hardware cannot skip arbitrary zero elements efficiently, and compact sparse formats add metadata and irregular access overhead", "Floating-point units automatically convert zero values into infinite loops", "Unstructured pruning forces the GPU driver to downclock memory bandwidth to prevent overheating", "The operating system kernel intercepts every zero multiplication and raises a hardware page fault"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: explain why unstructured sparsity often fails to accelerate inference on dense accelerator paths."]}, {"q": "Place the following steps in the correct execution sequence for processing a matrix multiplication on an accelerator with Sparse Tensor Cores using 2:4 structured sparsity:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (1) Fine-tune or prune the weight matrix to ensure exactly two non-zero values exist in every four-element contiguous group, (4) Compress the sparse weight matrix by storing only the non-zero values alongside metadata describing their positions, (2) Sparse Tensor Cores load compressed weights and decode metadata to gather matching input activation elements, (3) Multiply non-zero weights by gathered activations and accumulate into output partial sums on the sparse path. The sequence is necessary because the runtime path assumes weights already satisfy the 2:4 constraint and have been compressed with consistent metadata. Swapping compression ahead of pruning would encode the wrong pattern, and executing the sparse multiply before metadata-guided selection would leave the hardware without the positional information needed to match retained weights with the correct activations.", "type": "order", "items": ["Fine-tune or prune the weight matrix to ensure exactly two non-zero values exist in every four-element contiguous group", "Compress the sparse weight matrix by storing only the non-zero values alongside metadata describing their positions", "Sparse Tensor Cores load compressed weights and decode metadata to gather matching input activation elements", "Multiply non-zero weights by gathered activations and accumulate into output partial sums on the sparse path"], "hints": ["Think about: sequence the workflow for 2:4 structured sparse matrix multiplication from constraint formation through runtime sparse execution."]}, {"q": "An inference serving system processes dynamic batch sizes ranging from a batch of 1 to 32 concurrent decode requests, each contributing one token at the current step. Why does dynamic batching pose a significant challenge to runtime kernel selection and hardware efficiency?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Changing batch size changes shape, available reuse, and parallel work, so a kernel configuration that is effective at one batch can be inefficient at another. Small batches may emphasize low-latency execution and limited reuse, while larger batches can expose more parallelism and justify different tiling or tactic choices. The precision-switching claim is invented, Tensor Cores are not limited to powers of two in that way, and DRAM bank power-down is not the core runtime-kernel-selection issue described here.", "type": "single", "options": ["Optimal tile sizes, thread block configurations, and memory-reuse opportunities change across batch sizes, making a single static kernel inefficient across all shapes", "Dynamic batching forces the GPU to switch from FP16 to FP64 precision for even batch sizes", "Tensor Cores cannot execute matrix multiplication when batch dimensions are not powers of two", "The GPU memory controller must physically power down DRAM banks when batch size decreases"], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why dynamic batch sizes make a single static kernel configuration inefficient across inference requests."]}, {"q": "Memory allocated on the host CPU that is locked into physical RAM and prevents operating system paging, enabling direct DMA transfers over PCIe to the GPU, is called ____ host memory.", "explanation": "pinned (or page-locked). Pinned host memory allows GPU DMA engines to transfer data asynchronously across the PCIe bus without CPU staging copies or page faults.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["pinned", "pinned (or page locked)", "pinned (or page-locked)"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the role of pinned host memory in optimizing host-to-accelerator data transfers."]}, {"q": "How does an auto-tuning ML compiler (such as TVM/Ansor) differ from a traditional handwritten library approach (such as cuDNN) for kernel selection?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Auto-tuning compiler systems search parameterized spaces of schedules such as tiling, unrolling, and thread mapping, then rank or measure candidates to produce kernels specialized to the target and shape. Handwritten libraries also use parameters, heuristics, and benchmarking tactics, but their emphasis is expert-designed implementations rather than open-ended schedule search by the compiler. The host-CPU claim is false, the infinite-runtime-search claim misstates how libraries operate, and 1-bit quantization is unrelated.", "type": "single", "options": ["Auto-tuning compilers execute code only on the host CPU, whereas handwritten libraries run on GPUs", "Handwritten libraries search an infinite combinatorial loop space at runtime, whereas auto-tuning compilers use static heuristics", "Auto-tuning compilers explore large parameterized search spaces of loop transformations and tile sizes using cost models or measurements to generate custom kernels, whereas handwritten libraries emphasize expert-tuned implementations and heuristics", "Auto-tuning compilers require all tensors to be quantized to 1-bit integers"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: compare compiler-driven auto-tuning search with the expert-tuned implementation strategy emphasized by vendor libraries."]}, {"q": "In the iron law of ML systems decomposition ($\\text{Time} = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}} + \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}} + L_{\\text{lat}}$), the unaccelerated fraction $(1-p)$ is an immutable, fixed property of the model architecture regardless of the hardware intervention.", "explanation": "False. The unaccelerated fraction $(1-p)$ is defined specifically by the hardware or system intervention rather than being an immutable architectural constant. For example, if an upgrade improves only the tensor computation unit ($R_{\\text{peak}}$), data transfer ($D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW}$) acts as the unaccelerated fraction; however, if the memory subsystem or interconnect is also upgraded, data transfer time is reduced and the relative unaccelerated fraction shifts.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how hardware interventions redefine the accelerated versus unaccelerated components of execution time."]}, {"q": "On a modern mobile heterogeneous System-on-Chip (SoC) featuring big.LITTLE CPUs, a mobile GPU, and a dedicated NPU, which compute engine is typically the most energy-efficient choice for a supported INT8 convolution workload?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. For a supported INT8 convolution path, the NPU is designed specifically to execute quantized tensor operations with better energy efficiency than a general-purpose CPU or GPU path. The measured pipeline energy still depends on fallback operators, data movement, and thermal behavior, so specialization does not by itself determine the whole-system result. The big CPU core is less specialized for this workload, and the memory controller and hypervisor are not the units that execute the convolution itself.", "type": "single", "options": ["The high-performance 'big' CPU core running single-threaded FP32 instructions", "The out-of-order system memory controller", "The dedicated Neural Processing Unit (NPU) optimized for quantized INT8 tensor operations", "The host operating system virtualization hypervisor"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: classify which SoC engine is typically most energy-efficient for a supported INT8 convolution workload."]}, {"q": "Place the following computing milestones in chronological order (from earliest to most recent) as hardware evolved toward modern AI accelerators:", "explanation": "The correct order is (3) Integration of floating-point units (FPUs) and digital signal processors (DSPs) as discrete coprocessors -> (4) General-purpose programmable GPUs and SIMD instruction set extensions for 3D graphics and multimedia -> (2) Emergence of fixed-function media codecs and network processors for video/packet streaming -> (1) Introduction of dedicated Tensor Cores and TPUs for deep learning matrix operations. During the 1980s, discrete FPUs and DSPs accelerated arithmetic. The 1990s introduced SIMD extensions and early 3D GPUs. The 2000s expanded fixed-function media engines and network processors. The 2010s ushered in deep learning DSAs like Google TPUs (2015/2017) and NVIDIA Volta Tensor Cores (2017).", "type": "order", "items": ["General-purpose programmable GPUs and SIMD instruction set extensions for 3D graphics and multimedia", "Emergence of fixed-function media codecs and network processors for video/packet streaming", "Introduction of dedicated Tensor Cores and TPUs for deep learning matrix operations"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the historical evolution of hardware specialization from FPUs to modern AI accelerators."]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v1-11-99-test", "chapter": "v1-11", "minutes": 10}});
